# Clash Mini - AI Assistant Governance & Knowledge Entry

## Workspace & Session Context Rules (Non-blocking)
1. Start from this repository's portable six-in-one documentation architecture before editing code or config.
2. `.brain` junction (when present in workspace): inspect whether `.brain` resolves to the active session Artifact Directory; if refreshing or unlinking it, **never** run `Remove-Item -Recurse -Force` (which deletes target contents) — always unlink the junction safely via `cmd /c "if exist .brain rmdir .brain & mklink /j .brain \"<artifact_dir>\""` without blocking immediate user requests.
3. Cross-directory access: when investigating files outside the active workspace, request permission once on the parent directory and prefer consolidated `.py` helper scripts per [clash_mini_pitfalls.md](./clash_mini_pitfalls.md).

---

## Six-in-One Minimalist Documentation Architecture (DRY)
Every agent session MUST respect the single source of truth across these six documents:

1. **[.cursorrules](./.cursorrules) (Canonical Agent Entry)**: This file — entry point, governance rules, and modification sequence (`.clinerules` delegates directly here to avoid duplication).
2. **[clash_mini_pitfalls.md](./clash_mini_pitfalls.md) (The Law - Behavioral Redlines)**: Sole source of truth for AI behavioral rules, command boundaries, system safety, and historical mistakes.
3. **[clash_mini_agreements.md](./clash_mini_agreements.md) (The Contract - Product Specs & SOP)**: Sole source of truth for software specs, ports, UI layouts, and the release checklist SOP (Section VIII).
4. **[clash_mini_silent_release.md](./clash_mini_silent_release.md) (Silent Release Guidelines)**: Defines the non-interactive automated release workflow.
5. **[memory_summary.md](./.brain/memory_summary.md) (Session Memory)**: Temporary session notes, build directories, and cache-cleaning commands.
6. **[task.md](./.brain/task.md) (Active Task Lifecycle)**: Active task plan, checklist, and verification status (reset after milestone releases).

---

## Knowledge Maintenance & Safety Redlines
1. **Strict DRY**: Keep behavioral redlines exclusively in [clash_mini_pitfalls.md](./clash_mini_pitfalls.md) and product specs/SOP exclusively in [clash_mini_agreements.md](./clash_mini_agreements.md); link rather than duplicate.
2. **Report Before Silent Fixes**: Whenever discovering pre-existing mismatches, stale config, or unintended behavior outside the requested fix, report findings and options clearly to the user before altering unrelated systems.
3. **Commit Synchronization Law (`.husky/pre-commit`)**: Pure documentation commits may stand alone. Any commit modifying source or window configuration (`.ts` / `.tsx` / `.rs` / `tauri*.conf.json`) MUST update [clash_mini_agreements.md](./clash_mini_agreements.md) and stage it in the **same commit** (never bypass with `--no-verify`).
4. **High-Impact Doc Refactor Verification**: When reorganizing core rule or spec documents, verify preserved invariants (ports, UI constraints, `git diff` review).
5. **Live Proxy & Process Protection**: Never kill, stop, restart, or probe running `clash-mini`, `mini-mihomo`, `clash_verge_service`, `verge-mihomo`, `clash-verge`, or ports `10801` / `9098` / `33335` (`33336`). Proactive static checks (`pnpm web:build`, `cargo check`, `cargo clippy`, `pnpm test`, `pnpm cargo-test`) run freely.
6. **Release Pre-flight Checks**: Before tagging any release, verify `python verify.py` passes 100%, audit updated dependency commits in `Cargo.lock` / `pnpm-lock.yaml`, and confirm `extract_update_logs.mjs` matches the intended fixes.
